{
  "_comment": [
    "Issuer, currency and face value.",
    "Denomination codes are prefixed by kind because the same face value exists",
    "as both a coin and a note -- a US dollar is both, and they are different",
    "objects with different catalogs.",
    "A face value is one denomination however many designs carry it: a half",
    "dime and a nickel are both five cents, a gold dollar and a Morgan both a",
    "dollar. The design is the series.",
    "US denominations are those Congress authorized: the Coinage Act of 1792",
    "(half cent to eagle), the Acts of March 3, 1849 (double eagle), March 3,",
    "1851 and March 3, 1865 (three cents), February 21, 1853 (three dollars),",
    "April 22, 1864 (two cents) and March 3, 1875 (twenty cents); 31 U.S.C.",
    "5112 and Public Law 104-208 for the bullion coins' $25, $50 and $100.",
    "Notes: the Acts of July 17, 1862 and March 3, 1863 for fractional",
    "currency (3, 5, 10, 15, 25 and 50 cents); the Federal Reserve Board for",
    "$500 to $10,000, the largest note issued for public circulation. A",
    "pattern never authorized as a denomination (the $4 Stella) is not here."
  ],
